# Explore Hydrogen's Spectral and Transition Geometry

Hydrogen combines an infinite energy staircase, integer angular winding, and a transition network governed by parity. Begin with the visual story, then open the deeper panels when you want the mathematics.

## 1. The bound-state staircase

The Rydberg-unit levels are $E_n=-1/n^2$. Drag the cutoff to watch infinitely many negative levels crowd toward the ionization threshold at zero.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

{{visualization:0}}

Run the numerical table when exact level ordering and adjacent gaps are useful:

{{demo:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the spectral definitions and proofs</summary>

The modeled set is $\{-1/n^2:n\ge1\}\cup[0,\infty)$. Every bound level is negative because $n^2>0$. If $a<b$, reciprocal order gives $1/a^2>1/b^2$, hence $-1/a^2<-1/b^2$. Finally, $1/n^2	o0$, so zero lies in the closure of the bound levels while remaining outside their negative discrete subset. For background, see [spectral theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spectral_theory).

</details>

{{algorithm:0}}

## 2. Rotation turns winding into an eigenvalue

The mode $\psi_m(\phi)=e^{im\phi}$ closes after one revolution when $m$ is integral. Explore positive, negative, and zero winding.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{visualization:1}}

{{demo:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the angular-momentum calculation</summary>

Differentiating gives $d\psi_m/d\phi=im\psi_m$. Therefore $L_z\psi_m=-i(im\psi_m)=m\psi_m$. This is the azimuthal part of the spherical-harmonic construction. See [spherical harmonics](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spherical_harmonics) for broader context.

</details>

## 3. A two-colored graph of transitions

An orbital state $(\ell,m)$ satisfies $|m|\le\ell$. An edge obeys $\Delta\ell=\pm1$ and $|\Delta m|\le1$. Every edge crosses between even and odd $\ell$.

{{visualization:2}}

{{demo:2}}

{{algorithm:1}}

## 4. Path parity as an instant diagnostic

Choose endpoints and a proposed number of steps. The widget tests the necessary law $(\ell_a+\ell_b) mod2=k mod2$.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{algorithm:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal the walk theorem and no-odd-cycle consequence</summary>

Each edge changes $\ell$ by one and flips its parity. Induction over a path therefore shows that an odd number of edges gives opposite endpoint parity, while an even number gives equal parity. A closed path has identical endpoints and hence must have even length. This is the standard structure of a [bipartite graph](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bipartite_graph).

</details>

## Where the model ends

The spectral set is modeled directly; identifying it with the spectrum of the Coulomb Hamiltonian requires unbounded-operator analysis. Likewise, the transition conditions are encoded as graph edges rather than derived from dipole matrix elements. Those boundaries point directly toward the next research questions.

